---
import ToolLayout from "../../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro";
import { AgeCalculator } from "../../../components/calculators/AgeCalculator.js";
import { getToolByPath } from "../../../content/tools.js";

const tool = getToolByPath("/tools/calculators/age")!;
---

<ToolLayout
  tool={tool}
  purpose="Calculate a person's exact age as of today or any other date, broken down into years, months, and days."
>
  <AgeCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="details">
    <h2>How it's calculated</h2>
    <p>
      The calculator counts whole calendar years, then whole months, then whole days between the birth date and the
      "as of" date — the same way most people count age by hand. Dates are compared as calendar dates only (no time
      zone is applied), so the result doesn't shift depending on where you are in the world.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Someone born on June 15, 2000, measured as of January 15, 2024, is 23 years, 7 months, and 0 days old — the
      birthday for 2024 hasn't occurred yet relative to January, so the year count hasn't incremented.
    </p>

    <h2>Leap-day birthdays</h2>
    <p>
      For a birth date of February 29, this tool observes the anniversary on February 28 in years that aren't leap
      years, and on February 29 in years that are. This is one reasonable convention (the one most civil
      recordkeeping systems use); it is not the only valid interpretation.
    </p>

    <h2>Assumptions and limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The birth date must be on or before the "as of" date.</li>
      <li>Calculations use calendar dates (YYYY-MM-DD) with no time-of-day component, so results are identical regardless of your local time zone.</li>
      <li>"Total days lived" counts whole 24-hour calendar days between the two dates.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
